SLS has just been converted to using gzip, and the result uploaded
to tsx-11.mit.edu.  The savings in disks is as follows:

series b - 2 disks
series c - 1 disk
series x - 2 disks
series t - 2 disks
-----------------
total      7 disks

The size of the SLS distribution shrunk from 30 disks to 23.
Even more significant is that many disks now have spare room
within which pkgs can be updated, so hopefully, these massive
juggling acts won't be so frequent in the coming months.  I also
took the opportunity of rearranging the a series so that packages
that are likely to be updated are not on the a2 disk, which
is an image, and hence can not be easily updated (except atomically).

The new extension for packages is .tpz rather than .taz.
Sysinstall has been modified to support both types, but
I don't intend to use .taz anymore.    Gzip can also
uncompress the old compress archives, so that made the 
support rather easy.

If you just need to grab updates in the future, but haven't
upgraded to this release, you can grab gzip.taz, which
is in the SLS root, to obtain the gzip binary.

A number of other enhancements/fixes went in as well.  First,
when using the prompt-before-install option upon install,
the size of the compressed package is printed.   Also
a description of each package may be printed, if such a
description is contained in the file "diskXN" on the disk.
Really, fgrep is just used to search for "pkgname:".

Also, a number of binaries have been updated on the a2
disk:  mkfs, mkefs, menu, and ps (that is procps) are among them.
Yes menu has been fixed: I didn't think anyone used it till it broke.
Also added was "vi" so that an editor is on hand for system
recovery.

Also, disk a1 is no longer compressed.  Hopefully, that will
reduce one more FAQ from c.o.l.

Smalltalk now fits on the c series, even though it lost a disk!

Next, I intend to add documentation (info files) to the b series
so some of these will grow again, but this has definitely improved
the situation.  Particularly, since I now have room to actually
update a package without having to juggle things around.

Peter.


